   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to information on persons subjected to section 73-a of the public officers law and who hold policy-making positions",
    "basePrintNo": "S3810",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3810-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §100-a, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3810\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\ninformation on persons subjected to section 73-a of the public\nofficers law and who hold policy-making positions\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo identify how many policy-making positions are held by women in New\nYork State government.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the Executive Law by adding a new section 100-a\nwhich defines \"agency\" and requires the Secretary of State to identify\npersons subjected to the public officers law and who hold\npolicy-making positions at all state agencies. The compilation shall\nidentify by agency the persons subjected to the Public Officers Law\nSection 73-a and who hold policy-making positions, the length of time\nsuch positions have been held and a representation of policy-making\npositions held by women compared to policy-making positions held by\nmen. The Secretary of State shall keep the information in a public\nrecord available for inspection and updated annually on the Department\nof State's website.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nPolicy-making positions are a pipeline to higher office. Women have\nmade great strides in recent generations in becoming leaders in\ngovernment. There is, however, much more work to be done. A woman has\nnever served in New York State as governor, attorney general or\ncomptroller. Only two women from New York State have served in the\nUnited States Senate where only 20% of its members are women. The\nUnited States House of Representatives members are only 17.8% women.\nThe New York State Legislature members are less than 30% women.\n\nIdentifying whether women are appointed to policy-making positions and\nmaking that information readily available will expose where more work\nneeds to be done to promote women into leadership positions and\nreflect the reality that women are at least 50% of the general\npopulation.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-16:S.8078/A.9538(Seawright)- Passed Assembly referred to Rules\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nOn the first of April next succeeding the date on which it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
